  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering UI Architecture**\n\nI\'m incredibly impressed with the clean, dependency-free Jetpack Compose code. It\'s pure, efficient, and offline-ready, which is fantastic. Now, the main challenge is how to structure our UI composables. Should I use one unified file for all the screens, or pursue another option?\n\n\n**Structuring Screen Functions**\n\nOkay, I\'ve decided to create the screen functions separately. Grouping the screen functions will really help with rapid navigation state mapping, and will make things very nice to work with. I have been thinking about the layouts for the different screens, including a loading splash, and a login screen. Also, a dashboard, and finally a patient entry screen.\n\n\n**Designing Patient Entry Screen**\n\nI\'m focusing now on the Patient Entry Screen design. I\'m prioritizing data fields for patient demographics: name, age, gender, referred doctor, and contact information. I\'m planning to include a searchable test selection that instantly updates the total cost. The offline database integration via Room is key, with automatic QR payment modal generation on totals greater than zero, and a \"Send to Lab WhatsApp\" button.\n\n\n**Designing App Screens**\n\nI\'m now designing `SettingsScreen` with configurations for the lab merchant\'s UPI, WhatsApp numbers, admin security PIN, and database backup triggers. I\'m also considering instant language toggles. I\'ve decided to write all screens within a single file for cleaner maintenance and compilation. The layout should follow frontend design instructions, with Material 3 standards like generous paddings and edge-to-edge drawing. I\'m also ensuring the use of standard icons from Material Icons.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will create `/app/src/main/java/com/example/ui/screens/AppScreens.kt`. It contains the complete set of visual layouts for the pathology phlebotomist workflow: areawise Splash, login with offline capabilities, metric-heavy dashboard, fast medical patient record creator with smart autocalculator & voice simulate option, test directory management screen, analytical reporting panels, and customized administrative locks. This achieves premium, professional clinical interfaces following the Material Design 3 guidelines."
